# Batch files must reach Windows with CRLF endings.
#
# cmd.exe reads a running .bat by byte offset rather than buffering the whole
# file, and its line accounting assumes CRLF. An LF-only batch file mostly
# works, and then does not: `goto` can resume mid-line, and a label at the end
# of the file can be missed entirely. update.bat is built out of labels, so it
# is the one most exposed to this.
#
# Stored as LF in the repository, checked out as CRLF everywhere.
*.bat text eol=crlf

# Migrations are checksummed byte for byte, so their newlines must not change
# between machines.
#
# bin/console records sha256(file) when a migration is applied and compares it
# on every later run. Left to git's own detection, a Windows checkout rewrites
# LF to CRLF, every already-applied migration changes checksum at once, and
# `update.bat` prints a screen of "has changed since it was applied" warnings
# that mean nothing. The cost is not the noise: it is that a real warning —
# somebody genuinely editing an applied migration — becomes invisible among
# them.
*.sql text eol=lf

# The rest is left to git's own detection, except where a byte-exact file
# would be corrupted by newline translation.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.ico binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
